-- Per-profile display names for paired cubes.
--
-- A cube's row in `cubes` is keyed by MAC and holds exactly one record
-- per *physical* cube: hardware versions, gyro capability, vendor. Those
-- are facts about the hardware and are the same no matter who is holding
-- it, so they are deliberately shared — a cube re-paired under a second
-- profile keeps everything the app has learned about it.
--
-- A name is not a fact about the hardware. It is one person's label for
-- their cube, and two profiles on the same phone are two people. Storing
-- it on the `cubes` row meant profile A's rename followed the cube into
-- profile B the moment B paired it (`cubes.upsert` transfers `user_id`
-- but preserves the row, name included). This table separates the two:
-- `cubes.name` is the name the cube advertises over BLE, and a row here
-- is one profile's override of it.
--
-- Keyed by (user_id, mac) rather than by cubes.id on purpose. The MAC is
-- the physical cube's identity and outlives the `cubes` row — forgetting
-- and re-pairing a cube mints a fresh id — so a name keyed by MAC is
-- still the right name when the same cube comes back. It also means a
-- profile's name survives the cube being borrowed by another profile and
-- handed back.
--
-- `name` is NOT NULL: "this profile has no name of its own for the cube"
-- is the absence of a row, not a row holding NULL. One representation for
-- one state.

CREATE TABLE cube_names (
    user_id TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    mac TEXT NOT NULL,
    name TEXT NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, mac)
);

-- No separate user_id index: the composite primary key already creates a
-- unique index with user_id as its leading column, which is exactly what
-- a "all names for this profile" lookup walks.

-- No SELECT of its own. Reads always go through `cubes.selectByUser`,
-- which LEFT JOINs this table, so the paired list and the names it shows
-- can never be assembled from two reads taken a moment apart.

put:
INSERT INTO cube_names (user_id, mac, name) VALUES (:userId, :mac, :name)
ON CONFLICT(user_id, mac) DO UPDATE SET name = excluded.name;

-- Drop this profile's override. The cube then shows whatever name it
-- advertises again, which is what clearing the rename field means.
remove:
DELETE FROM cube_names WHERE user_id = :userId AND mac = :mac;

-- Drop the override belonging to the profile that currently owns the
-- given cube row. Used when forgetting a cube: the caller has an id, and
-- both the MAC and the owning profile hang off it.
--
-- Must run BEFORE `cubes.deleteById` — the subqueries read the row it
-- deletes. `DevicesRepository.forget` wraps the pair in a transaction so
-- the ordering can't be got wrong by accident.
removeForCubeId:
DELETE FROM cube_names
WHERE mac = (SELECT mac FROM cubes WHERE id = :id)
  AND user_id = (SELECT user_id FROM cubes WHERE id = :id);

deleteAllForUser:
DELETE FROM cube_names WHERE user_id = :userId;
